\flmHeading[toc=true]
\section{Introduction}{\flmLabels{\label{ref1}}}

This document exercises every standard feature of build step 1: headings,
paragraphs, the base formatting commands, math, user definitions and
references. It is version 1.0.

\flmHeading[toc=true]
\subsection{Formatting}{\flmLabels{\label{ref2}}}

Some \emph{emphasized}, \textbf{bold}, \textit{italic} and \texttt{monospace}
text --- with a dash, an ellipsis... and "quotes" resolved by typography.
%% A comment vanishes (the language's default comment prefix is `%%`).

\flmHeading[toc=true]
\subsubsection{Math}{\flmLabels{}}

Inline math \(x_1 + \alpha\) and display math \[ \int_0^1 f(x)\,dx = 1 \] then
two environments:

\begin{equation}
  E = mc^2
\end{equation}

\begin{align*}
  a &= b \\
  c &= d
\end{align*}

\flmHeading[toc=true]
\subsubsection{Verbatim}{\flmLabels{}}

Inline \EscVerb[formatcom=\flmFmtVRB{verbtext}]{a\{b\}\\c} text, the code \EscVerb[formatcom=\flmFmtVRB{verbcode}]{f\(x\)\ \=\ 1} and the
identifier \EscVerb[formatcom=\flmFmtVRB{verba}]{struct}, then \EscVerb[formatcom=\flmFmtVRB{verbtext}]{raw\ \{text\}}
in the same paragraph, and a code block:

\begin{flmVerbatimCode}[python]
def f(x):
    return {x} + 1
\end{flmVerbatimCode}

\flmHeading[toc=true]
\paragraph{Definitions}{\flmLabels{}}

\flmUDhello{World} from a user macro, and \begin{flmUEnote}a user environment\end{flmUEnote}
in the same paragraph.

\flmHeading[toc=true]
\subparagraph{References}{\flmLabels{}}

See \flmRef{ref1}, \flmRef[noprefix]{ref2} and \flmRef{ref3}.

\flmHeading[toc=true]
